/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  evaporator09_nacl_sucrose_brine  --  a liquor with TWO nonvolatiles, and a
                                       model that was fitted on one of them.

  evaporator06 concentrates a clean NaCl brine: everything dissolved is the
  salt, so "the molality" is unambiguous.  This case adds sucrose --- an
  ordinary situation, a brine that also carries organics --- and the word
  stops being unambiguous:

      m(all dissolved) = 4.920 mol/kg      the COLLIGATIVE molality
      m(NaCl)          = 3.968 mol/kg      the Pitzer model's own basis

  The boiling-point elevation is computed from the water activity, and the
  Pitzer parameters behind that activity were regressed on sodium chloride in
  water.  Handing them 4.920 asks them about a solution they have never seen
  and reports the answer as though it were the brine's.  So the unit uses
  3.968 and SAYS what it left out:

      [unmodelledSolutes] the liquid carries 0.952 mol/kg of dissolved
                          sucrose -- not 'NaCl', and not represented by the
                          single-salt electrolyte model ... their own
                          contribution is NOT included.

  Which is the honest position: the sucrose really does depress the vapour
  pressure, and this package has no term for it.  A student can see the size
  of what is missing (a fifth of the dissolved matter) instead of finding it
  quietly absorbed into a salt correlation.

  The colligative reading is not thrown away --- it is published as the
  `molality` KPI, beside `molality_salt`.  Two names, because they are two
  quantities.

  The same distinction now governs the heat of dilution and the
  extrapolation guard: L_phi and its validity window are the salt's, so the
  molality tested against them is the salt's.

  The sentence itself is not this unit's.  It is measured and worded in
  thermo/electrolyte/UnmodelledSolutes and printed identically by the
  crystallisation saturation --- see crystalliser09, where a wine lee holds
  bitartrate while the package models potassium chloride.  Same omission,
  same words, one place to correct them.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoSolve;
description   "Evaporating a brine that also carries sugar: the electrolyte BPE is computed on the salt molality, and the rest is announced, not absorbed";
verbosity     3;
